ce definitions; planetary figures are equatorial diameters.", "sources": ["https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm", "https://www.toureiffel.paris/en/the-monument/key-figures"], "id": "how-much-ish-13", "version": 1}, {"kind": "scale", "prompt": "Resize Mercury to its real diameter relative to Venus.", "target": {"name": "Mercury", "dimension": "diameter", "shape": "sphere"}, "reference": {"name": "Venus", "dimension": "diameter", "shape": "sphere"}, "bounds": [0.05, 20], "answer": 0.40308988764044945, "explanation": "Mercury: 4,879,000 m diameter. Venus: 12,104,000 m diameter. The ratio is 0.403×. Measurements use the source definitions; planetary figures are equatorial diameters.", "sources": ["https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/"], "id": "how-much-ish-14", "version": 1}, {"kind": "scale", "prompt": "Resize Liberty’s right arm to its real length relative to Liberty’s hand.", "target": {"name": "Liberty’s right arm", "dimension": "length", "shape": "arm"}, "reference": {"name": "Liberty’s hand", "dimension": "length", "shape": "hand"}, "bounds": [0.05, 20], "answer": 2.56, "explanation": "Liberty’s right arm: 13 m length. Liberty’s hand: 5 m length. The ratio is 2.56×. Measurements use the source definitions; planetary figures are equatorial diameters.", "sources": ["https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm"], "id": "how-much-ish-15", "version": 1}]'::jsonb);
